(1275919, 161088, 12356, '<td valign="top">This is one of those rare instances ...  where it is much more than just a photo ... as others have said:  look at this as you listen to some acoustical music;  ..   remember your first time of trying to get the chords right ... or remember trying to help <b>someone else</b> to get the chords right .... ''tis this kind of shot ..  that goes far beyond just being great photography, but captures a far deeper and broader spectrum of life ...  makes the shot rise above others ....  Thanks for sharing!</td>', 'This is one of those rare instances ...  where it is much more than just a photo ... as others have said:  look at this as you listen to some acoustical music;  ..   remember your first time of trying to get the chords right ... or remember trying to help someone else to get the chords right .... ''tis this kind of shot ..  that goes far beyond just being great photography, but captures a far deeper and broader spectrum of life ...  makes the shot rise above others ....  Thanks for sharing!', '2005-04-06 20:31:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(1275961, 161287, 12356, '<td valign="top">Thanks for taking time to comment.  I happened upon this mimosa tree while chasing a sunrise to the river almost a year ago, and re-visited occasionally since.  Went again on Easter morning, and was delighted to find the very beginning of this year''s buds, with the sunrise in the background.  Thought it fit, since buds are a beginning, as are sunrises, as is Easter....  Hope to get some more to share later.  My first experience with this tree  was with a Kodak DX6490, where I was trying out the 10x optical, and was surprised to discover how much fun it is to ''get intimate'' with a mimosa blossom in full glory.  *grin*  This time, I have a new-to-me- Olympus C740UZ, and was trying it out on an old friend (the tree).  The Kodak has a better resolution, but regularly requires the use of the sharing dock to unload its photos, and I cannot always do that in the field.  Thus am hoping to move to the Olympus, to avoid that issue.</td>', 'Thanks for taking time to comment.  I happened upon this mimosa tree while chasing a sunrise to the river almost a year ago, and re-visited occasionally since.  Went again on Easter morning, and was delighted to find the very beginning of this year''s buds, with the sunrise in the background.  Thought it fit, since buds are a beginning, as are sunrises, as is Easter....  Hope to get some more to share later.  My first experience with this tree  was with a Kodak DX6490, where I was trying out the 10x optical, and was surprised to discover how much fun it is to ''get intimate'' with a mimosa blossom in full glory.  *grin*  This time, I have a new-to-me- Olympus C740UZ, and was trying it out on an old friend (the tree).  The Kodak has a better resolution, but regularly requires the use of the sharing dock to unload its photos, and I cannot always do that in the field.  Thus am hoping to move to the Olympus, to avoid that issue.', '2005-04-06 20:49:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(1275980, 161174, 19363, '<td valign="top">Thanks Tash and everyone else who commented.<br/> <br/>Seems a bit weird that I got 27 comments (most of which are very favourable), the pic has been selected as a favourite by 2 other users and it only scraped in over a 5 - most of the week it was below 5.<br/> <br/>But, hey, what the heck do I know? - Onwards and (hopefully) upwards!<br/> <br/>Scott</td>', 'Thanks Tash and everyone else who commented. Seems a bit weird that I got 27 comments (most of which are very favourable), the pic has been selected as a favourite by 2 other users and it only scraped in over a 5 - most of the week it was below 5. But, hey, what the heck do I know? - Onwards and (hopefully) upwards! Scott', '2005-04-06 20:55:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(1275993, 160409, 26849, '<td valign="top">My mistake = Hollywood''s history is NOT well known. <br/> <br/>I thought almost everyone knew this sign was first put up to promote a real estate development. Then when the movies made Hollywood world famous they kept it as a landmark. The sign originally said "Hollywoodland" but a landslide ruined some of the letters. They only restore the Hollywood portion.<br/> <br/>In essence this sign was there at the beginning and an important part of Hollywood''s early history. So much for this lesson.<br/> <br/>Other comments:<br/>"Common" shot. It''s a rare shot. As some may know, L.A. just had it''s 2nd highest all time rain season. You will not see the hills around the sign this green in normal years. I did bump up the saturation during Raw conversion but only about as far as the camera would allow.<br/> <br/>Tighter crop? I would have liked it better myself but I wanted the California Golden Poppy''s for added color and the house to tie in the real estate development concept. The poppy''s were in a restricted area.<br/> <br/>Long Title? Maybe not long enough to give the history lesson. ;)<br/> <br/>Knew it wouldn''t ribbon but thought it would be interesting for many to see how green it is here after this last rainy season. <br/> <br/>Estimated it would score in the 5''s at least. It started out with about 20 people giving it a 3 or less the first night and averaged 3.1. Then it gradually moved up to this current score. What a disappointment.<br/></td>', 'My mistake = Hollywood''s history is NOT well known.  I thought almost everyone knew this sign was first put up to promote a real estate development. Then when the movies made Hollywood world famous they kept it as a landmark. The sign originally said "Hollywoodland" but a landslide ruined some of the letters. They only restore the Hollywood portion. In essence this sign was there at the beginning and an important part of Hollywood''s early history. So much for this lesson. Other comments:"Common" shot. It''s a rare shot. As some may know, L.A. just had it''s 2nd highest all time rain season. You will not see the hills around the sign this green in normal years. I did bump up the saturation during Raw conversion but only about as far as the camera would allow. Tighter crop? I would have liked it better myself but I wanted the California Golden Poppy''s for added color and the house to tie in the real estate development concept. The poppy''s were in a restricted area. Long Title? Maybe not long enough to give the history lesson. ;) Knew it wouldn''t ribbon but thought it would be interesting for many to see how green it is here after this last rainy season.  Estimated it would score in the 5''s at least. It started out with about 20 people giving it a 3 or less the first night and averaged 3.1. Then it gradually moved up to this current score. What a disappointment.', '2005-04-06 20:59:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(1275994, 161295, 19116, '<td valign="top">Thanks everybody for all the great comments.  This is indeed homebrew- a brown ale that came out pretty good, if I do say so myself.  The glass in front is not homebrew.  I didn''t know how long it was going to have to sit out and I didn''t want to waste one.  The differing fill levels kind of bothered me, but I didn''t have enough full bottles left to be able to pick and choose.<br/> <br/>The strange shadow in the glass is not reflected, its actually a shadow cast on the background by one of my lamps.  Didn''t notice it at the time the shot was taken.<br/> <br/>I kinda liked the highlights- though I guess blowing out highlights IS the cardinal sin in digital photography.  I find it interesting that nobody commented that the glass looked dirty (it wasn''t, but backlighting can be unkind).  That was what bothered me most about the photo.<br/> <br/>Thanks again for all the helpful comments.</td>', 'Thanks everybody for all the great comments.  This is indeed homebrew- a brown ale that came out pretty good, if I do say so myself.  The glass in front is not homebrew.  I didn''t know how long it was going to have to sit out and I didn''t want to waste one.  The differing fill levels kind of bothered me, but I didn''t have enough full bottles left to be able to pick and choose. The strange shadow in the glass is not reflected, its actually a shadow cast on the background by one of my lamps.  Didn''t notice it at the time the shot was taken. I kinda liked the highlights- though I guess blowing out highlights IS the cardinal sin in digital photography.  I find it interesting that nobody commented that the glass looked dirty (it wasn''t, but backlighting can be unkind).  That was what bothered me most about the photo. Thanks again for all the helpful comments.', '2005-04-06 21:00:05.000000', NULL, 0),
